WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
This synthetic MAST allergy test measures 69 allergens.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
OCR reads 714 for dog dander.
The source shows 7.14 IU/mL, class 3.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The simulated patient reports worse symptoms after dog contact
and no symptoms after eating orange.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:31.000
For house dust mites, the questionnaire asks about timing.
The simulated answer is yes to worse symptoms at night or early morning.

5
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.000
The simulated patient says symptoms improve
after a few days away from home.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Do symptoms worsen when shaking out bedding or dusting?
The simulated answer is yes.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The simulated patient often sees cats
but does not live with one.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000
How often does the simulated patient see cats?
The answer is about once a week.

9
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Do symptoms worsen with more cat contact?
The simulated answer is no.

10
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:14.000
The simulated patient selects “No seasonal difference”
when asked which seasons are worse.

11
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:22.000
The spring tree pollen question asks about nose and eye symptoms
from March to May. This case has no recorded answer.

12
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:30.000
The grass pollen question asks about symptoms near lawns
in May and June. This case has no recorded answer.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
The weed pollen question asks about nose and eye symptoms
from August to October. This case has no recorded answer.

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Dog dander and orange are both class 3. The patient guidance
relates each test result to the reported symptoms.

15
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:54.000
These three original Korean cards come from the 14-card deck
generated for the synthetic case.

16
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:02.000
The FHIR R4 bundle links one Patient, 69 Observations
and one DiagnosticReport, for a total of 71 resources.
